<https://riskfolio-graphrag.io/kg/v2/Tutorial%2015%20-%20Mean%20Entropic%20Value%20at%20Risk%20(EVaR)%20Optimization.ipynb::chunk:4> a rf:Chunk ;
    rf:chunk_index 4 ;
    rf:chunk_kind "example_section" ;
    rf:content """## 2. Estimating Mean EVaR Portfolios

### 2.1 Calculating the portfolio that optimize EVaR ratio.

The Entropic Value at Risk which is a new risk measure introduced by Ahmadi-Javid (2012) and is defined as:

$$
\\text{EVaR}_{\\alpha}(X) = \\inf_{z>0} \\left \\{z\\log \\left ( \\frac{1}{\\alpha} M_{X} (\\frac{1}{z}) \\right ) \\right \\}
$$

Where $M_{X} (t) = \\text{E} [e^{tX}]$ is the moment generating function and $\\alpha \\in [0,1]$ is the significance level.
    
In a similar way than Markowitz model, the mean EVaR model can be expressed as one of the following problems:

$$
\\begin{aligned}
& \\min_{w,\\, z} & & \\text{EVaR}_{\\alpha}(r w)\\\\
& & & 1^{T}w = 0 \\\\
& & & w \\geq 0 \\\\
\\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\\begin{aligned}
& \\max_{w,\\, z} & & \\mu w - \\lambda \\text{EVaR}_{\\alpha}(r w)\\\\
& & & 1^{T}w = 0 \\\\
& & & w \\geq 0 \\\\
\\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\\begin{aligned}
& \\max_{w,\\, z} & & \\frac{\\mu w - r_{f}}{ \\text{EVaR}_{\\alpha}(r w)}\\\\
& & & 1^{T}w = 0 \\\\
& & & w \\geq 0 \\\\
\\end{aligned}
$$

Where $z$ is the factor""" ;
    rf:content_hash "c248ddb9be229d6f7814590c6c5dd0ed2ec005157e8fa372d80ec8e2b4b133b1" ;
    rf:line_end 4 ;
    rf:line_start 4 ;
    rf:relative_path "Tutorial 15 - Mean Entropic Value at Risk (EVaR) Optimization.ipynb" ;
    rf:section "example_section" ;
    rf:source_path "/Users/et/Desktop/Data_Projects/Riskfolio-Lib/examples/Tutorial 15 - Mean Entropic Value at Risk (EVaR) Optimization.ipynb" .

<https://riskfolio-graphrag.io/kg/v2/Tutorial%2015%20-%20Mean%20Entropic%20Value%20at%20Risk%20(EVaR)%20Optimization.ipynb::chunk:5> a rf:Chunk ;
    rf:chunk_index 5 ;
    rf:chunk_kind "example_section" ;
    rf:content """aR}_{\\alpha}(r w)}\\\\
& & & 1^{T}w = 0 \\\\
& & & w \\geq 0 \\\\
\\end{aligned}
$$

Where $z$ is the factor of EVaR, $w$ are the weights of assets, $\\mu$ is the mean vector, $\\lambda$ is the risk aversion factor, $r$ is the returns matrix and $r_{f}$ the risk free rate. For more details see this __[link](https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=3792520)__.

It is recommended to use MOSEK to optimize EVaR, due to it requires exponential cone programming to solve the problem.

Instructions to install MOSEK are in this __[link](https://docs.mosek.com/9.2/install/installation.html)__, is better to install using Anaconda. Also you will need a license, I recommend you that ask for an academic license __[here](https://www.mosek.com/products/academic-licenses/)__.""" ;
    rf:content_hash "6ab8e71348b8e882757a0fdc62561b9880cd41b1a2cb3113f92e7f4e84dd9f0c" ;
    rf:line_end 4 ;
    rf:line_start 4 ;
    rf:relative_path "Tutorial 15 - Mean Entropic Value at Risk (EVaR) Optimization.ipynb" ;
    rf:section "example_section" ;
    rf:source_path "/Users/et/Desktop/Data_Projects/Riskfolio-Lib/examples/Tutorial 15 - Mean Entropic Value at Risk (EVaR) Optimization.ipynb" .

<https://riskfolio-graphrag.io/kg/v2/Tutorial%2015%20-%20Mean%20Entropic%20Value%20at%20Risk%20(EVaR)%20Optimization.ipynb::chunk:6> a rf:Chunk ;
    rf:chunk_index 6 ;
    rf:chunk_kind "example_cell" ;
    rf:content """import riskfolio as rp

# Building the portfolio object
port = rp.Portfolio(returns=Y)

# Calculating optimum portfolio

# Select method and estimate input parameters:

method_mu='hist' # Method to estimate expected returns based on historical data.
method_cov='hist' # Method to estimate covariance matrix based on historical data.

port.assets_stats(method_mu=method_mu, method_cov=method_cov)

# Estimate optimal portfolio:

port.solvers = ['MOSEK'] # It is recommended to use mosek when optimizing EVaR 
port.alpha = 0.05 # Significance level for CVaR, EVaR y CDaR 
model='Classic' # Could be Classic (historical), BL (Black Litterman) or FM (Factor Model)
rm = 'EVaR' # Risk measure used, this time will be EVaR
obj = 'Sharpe' # Objective function, could be MinRisk, MaxRet, Utility or Sharpe
hist = True # Use historical scenarios for risk measures that depend on scenarios
rf = 0 # Risk free rate
l = 0 # Risk aversion factor, only useful when obj is 'Utility'

w = port.optimization(model=mod""" ;
    rf:content_hash "c4a650d6b8b4581da44da8b3cb30ffff202f6be2c74acfb42c8e18f188c2ce9c" ;
    rf:line_end 5 ;
    rf:line_start 5 ;
    rf:relative_path "Tutorial 15 - Mean Entropic Value at Risk (EVaR) Optimization.ipynb" ;
    rf:section "example_cell" ;
    rf:source_path "/Users/et/Desktop/Data_Projects/Riskfolio-Lib/examples/Tutorial 15 - Mean Entropic Value at Risk (EVaR) Optimization.ipynb" .

