<https://riskfolio-graphrag.io/kg/v2/Tutorial%2020%20-%20Mean%20Risk%20Optimization%20using%20Black%20Litterman%20and%20Risk%20Factors%20Models.ipynb::chunk:32> a rf:Chunk ;
    rf:chunk_index 32 ;
    rf:chunk_kind "example_section" ;
    rf:content "### 3.3 Building Portfolios with mean vector and covariance matrix from Black Litterman with Factors." ;
    rf:content_hash "6ee101e7160e9eba2553b96eb13a9860e7c516da0fa801079cb10ff760cb4b67" ;
    rf:line_end 28 ;
    rf:line_start 28 ;
    rf:relative_path "Tutorial 20 - Mean Risk Optimization using Black Litterman and Risk Factors Models.ipynb" ;
    rf:section "example_section" ;
    rf:source_path "/Users/et/Desktop/Data_Projects/Riskfolio-Lib/examples/Tutorial 20 - Mean Risk Optimization using Black Litterman and Risk Factors Models.ipynb" .

<https://riskfolio-graphrag.io/kg/v2/Tutorial%2020%20-%20Mean%20Risk%20Optimization%20using%20Black%20Litterman%20and%20Risk%20Factors%20Models.ipynb::chunk:33> a rf:Chunk ;
    rf:chunk_index 33 ;
    rf:chunk_kind "example_cell" ;
    rf:content """########################################################################
# Building Portfolio Object
########################################################################

# Building the portfolio object
port = rp.Portfolio(returns=Y)

# Calculating optimum portfolio

# Select method and estimate input parameters:

method_mu='hist' # Method to estimate expected returns based on historical data.
method_cov='hist' # Method to estimate covariance matrix based on historical data.

port.assets_stats(method_mu=method_mu,
                  method_cov=method_cov)

port.factors = X
port.factors_stats(method_mu=method_mu,
                   method_cov=method_cov,
                   B=loadings,
                   const=True)""" ;
    rf:content_hash "8fe732132f68ec7c71a32bf29449978093a40bf6fd7f76d7b4fdbe7705cf6586" ;
    rf:line_end 29 ;
    rf:line_start 29 ;
    rf:relative_path "Tutorial 20 - Mean Risk Optimization using Black Litterman and Risk Factors Models.ipynb" ;
    rf:section "example_cell" ;
    rf:source_path "/Users/et/Desktop/Data_Projects/Riskfolio-Lib/examples/Tutorial 20 - Mean Risk Optimization using Black Litterman and Risk Factors Models.ipynb" .

<https://riskfolio-graphrag.io/kg/v2/Tutorial%2020%20-%20Mean%20Risk%20Optimization%20using%20Black%20Litterman%20and%20Risk%20Factors%20Models.ipynb::chunk:34> a rf:Chunk ;
    rf:chunk_index 34 ;
    rf:chunk_kind "example_cell" ;
    rf:content """########################################################################
# Calculating optimum portfolios
########################################################################

port.alpha = 0.05
rm = 'MV' # Risk measure used, this time will be variance
obj = 'Sharpe' # Objective function, could be MinRisk, MaxRet, Utility or Sharpe
hist = False # False: BL covariance and risk factors scenarios
             # True: historical covariance and scenarios
             # 2: risk factors covariance and scenarios
rf = 0 # Risk free rate
l = 0 # Risk aversion factor, only useful when obj is 'Utility'

w_fm = port.optimization(model='FM', rm=rm, obj=obj, rf=rf, l=l, hist=hist) 

# Estimate Portfolio weights using Black Litterman Bayesian Model:
port.blfactors_stats(flavor='BLB',
                     B=loadings,
                     P_f=P_f,
                     Q_f=Q_f/252,
                     rf=0,
                     delta=None,
                     eq=True,
                     const=True""" ;
    rf:content_hash "be260de0e1fe5ecbe0f6ecb0970b98c5722f3827552e55986e9c6568f4b3e3a7" ;
    rf:line_end 30 ;
    rf:line_start 30 ;
    rf:relative_path "Tutorial 20 - Mean Risk Optimization using Black Litterman and Risk Factors Models.ipynb" ;
    rf:section "example_cell" ;
    rf:source_path "/Users/et/Desktop/Data_Projects/Riskfolio-Lib/examples/Tutorial 20 - Mean Risk Optimization using Black Litterman and Risk Factors Models.ipynb" .

<https://riskfolio-graphrag.io/kg/v2/Tutorial%2020%20-%20Mean%20Risk%20Optimization%20using%20Black%20Litterman%20and%20Risk%20Factors%20Models.ipynb::chunk:35> a rf:Chunk ;
    rf:chunk_index 35 ;
    rf:chunk_kind "example_cell" ;
    rf:content """rf=0,
                     delta=None,
                     eq=True,
                     const=True,
                     method_mu=method_mu,
                     method_cov=method_cov)

w_blb = port.optimization(model='BL_FM', rm=rm, obj=obj, rf=rf, l=l, hist=hist)

# Estimate Portfolio weights using Augmented Black Litterman Model:
port.blfactors_stats(flavor='ABL',
                     B=loadings,
                     P_f=P_f,
                     Q_f=Q_f/252,
                     rf=0,
                     delta=None,
                     eq=True,
                     const=True,
                     method_mu=method_mu,
                     method_cov=method_cov)

w_abl = port.optimization(model='BL_FM', rm=rm, obj=obj, rf=rf, l=l, hist=hist)

ws = pd.concat([w_fm, w_blb, w_abl], axis=1)
ws.columns = ['Pure Factors', 'Bayesian BL', 'Augmented BL']

display(ws.style.format("{:.4%}").background_gradient(cmap='YlGn'))""" ;
    rf:content_hash "315dcccb5a2b862953c3751b184126caef416dfd9eb7ccac22be87c54cd00cdb" ;
    rf:line_end 30 ;
    rf:line_start 30 ;
    rf:relative_path "Tutorial 20 - Mean Risk Optimization using Black Litterman and Risk Factors Models.ipynb" ;
    rf:section "example_cell" ;
    rf:source_path "/Users/et/Desktop/Data_Projects/Riskfolio-Lib/examples/Tutorial 20 - Mean Risk Optimization using Black Litterman and Risk Factors Models.ipynb" .

<https://riskfolio-graphrag.io/kg/v2/Tutorial%2020%20-%20Mean%20Risk%20Optimization%20using%20Black%20Litterman%20and%20Risk%20Factors%20Models.ipynb::chunk:36> a rf:Chunk ;
    rf:chunk_index 36 ;
    rf:chunk_kind "example_section" ;
    rf:content """We can see that the we got a messsage that the covariance matrix is not a positive definite matrix, this is common when we work with views on assets or risk factors. In this case, Riskfolio-Lib replace the negative eigenvalues of covariance matrix with zeros and reconstruct the covariance matrix. The problem with this approach is common that the weights that we will get are highly concetrated in few assets. 

We can see that in this case the Black Litterman Bayesian model creates a more diversified portfolio than the Augmented Black Litterman model.

Other approach is using the mean vector estimated with Black Litterman with Factors and the covariance matrix that we get from historical returns or a factor model.""" ;
    rf:content_hash "d9f52f9080811823636c0bc21e751869df368d483b18df42f96139282f49ac5e" ;
    rf:line_end 31 ;
    rf:line_start 31 ;
    rf:relative_path "Tutorial 20 - Mean Risk Optimization using Black Litterman and Risk Factors Models.ipynb" ;
    rf:section "example_section" ;
    rf:source_path "/Users/et/Desktop/Data_Projects/Riskfolio-Lib/examples/Tutorial 20 - Mean Risk Optimization using Black Litterman and Risk Factors Models.ipynb" .

