<https://riskfolio-graphrag.io/kg/v2/Tutorial%2050%20-%20Mean%20Relativistic%20Value%20at%20Risk%20Range%20(RVRG)%20Optimization.ipynb::chunk:4> a rf:Chunk ;
    rf:chunk_index 4 ;
    rf:chunk_kind "example_section" ;
    rf:content """## 2. Estimating Mean RLVaR Range Portfolios

### 2.1 Calculating the portfolio that optimize RLVaR Range ratio.

It is recommended to use MOSEK to optimize RLVaR Range, due to it requires exponential cone programming to solve the problem.

Instructions to install MOSEK are in this __[link](https://docs.mosek.com/9.2/install/installation.html)__, is better to install using Anaconda. Also you will need a license, I recommend you that ask for an academic license __[here](https://www.mosek.com/products/academic-licenses/)__.""" ;
    rf:content_hash "e882e6ae0975e90ca77767c4a428616ff319e6b01b46d139a7a91f5e8be817d8" ;
    rf:line_end 4 ;
    rf:line_start 4 ;
    rf:relative_path "Tutorial 50 - Mean Relativistic Value at Risk Range (RVRG) Optimization.ipynb" ;
    rf:section "example_section" ;
    rf:source_path "/Users/et/Desktop/Data_Projects/Riskfolio-Lib/examples/Tutorial 50 - Mean Relativistic Value at Risk Range (RVRG) Optimization.ipynb" .

<https://riskfolio-graphrag.io/kg/v2/Tutorial%2050%20-%20Mean%20Relativistic%20Value%20at%20Risk%20Range%20(RVRG)%20Optimization.ipynb::chunk:5> a rf:Chunk ;
    rf:chunk_index 5 ;
    rf:chunk_kind "example_cell" ;
    rf:content """import riskfolio as rp

# Building the portfolio object
port = rp.Portfolio(returns=Y)

# Calculating optimum portfolio

# Select method and estimate input parameters:

method_mu='hist' # Method to estimate expected returns based on historical data.
method_cov='hist' # Method to estimate covariance matrix based on historical data.

port.assets_stats(method_mu=method_mu, method_cov=method_cov)

# Estimate optimal portfolio:

port.solvers = ['MOSEK'] # It is recommended to use mosek when optimizing EVaR 
port.alpha = 0.05 # Significance level for EVRG 
model='Classic' # Could be Classic (historical), BL (Black Litterman) or FM (Factor Model)
rm = 'RVRG' # Risk measure used
obj = 'Sharpe' # Objective function, could be MinRisk, MaxRet, Utility or Sharpe
hist = True # Use historical scenarios for risk measures that depend on scenarios
rf = 0 # Risk free rate
l = 0 # Risk aversion factor, only useful when obj is 'Utility'

w = port.optimization(model=model, rm=rm, obj=obj, rf=rf, l=l, hist=""" ;
    rf:content_hash "b7b4afa05ef2c807dd99dec67edceaf34253c0be66e3c12d7996f6e66d956971" ;
    rf:line_end 5 ;
    rf:line_start 5 ;
    rf:relative_path "Tutorial 50 - Mean Relativistic Value at Risk Range (RVRG) Optimization.ipynb" ;
    rf:section "example_cell" ;
    rf:source_path "/Users/et/Desktop/Data_Projects/Riskfolio-Lib/examples/Tutorial 50 - Mean Relativistic Value at Risk Range (RVRG) Optimization.ipynb" .

<https://riskfolio-graphrag.io/kg/v2/Tutorial%2050%20-%20Mean%20Relativistic%20Value%20at%20Risk%20Range%20(RVRG)%20Optimization.ipynb::chunk:6> a rf:Chunk ;
    rf:chunk_index 6 ;
    rf:chunk_kind "example_cell" ;
    rf:content """y useful when obj is 'Utility'

w = port.optimization(model=model, rm=rm, obj=obj, rf=rf, l=l, hist=hist)

display(w.T)""" ;
    rf:content_hash "4306c7c8c32a7faaa2da3efb6fd3aa394e4afa95b4216972e2feefe68c41b018" ;
    rf:line_end 5 ;
    rf:line_start 5 ;
